,"content_type":"application/pgp-signature","state":"uploaded","size":566,"digest":"sha256:01ccc98a4ee847621391f2e571ad69443127cf73482ae5d316c55d46bf15fbcc","download_count":9,"created_at":"2026-10-31T10:19:10Z","updated_at":"2026-10-31T10:19:10Z","browser_download_url":"https://github.com/jdx/mise/releases/download/v2026.10.25/v2026.10.25.tar.gz.sig"}],"tarball_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mise/tarball/v2026.10.25","zipball_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mise/zipball/v2026.10.25","body":"`mise tasks` can now add, change and remove tasks in a chosen config file without an editor, and `mise watch --once` waits for one change, runs the tasks and exits. Both suit scripts and coding agents. The release also adds opt-in env caching for configs with redacted values, and fixes concurrent installs, lockfiles in symlinked config dirs, slow reshims and signed macOS app bundles.\n\n## Added\n\n- **`mise tasks set` and `mise tasks rm`, plus more options for `mise tasks add`.** [#15213](https://github.com/jdx/mise/pull/15213) (@jdx)\n  - `add`, `set` and `rm` take the same `--env`, `--global` and `--path` flags as `mise use`, so you can pick the file they write to.\n  - `add` and `set` take new task fields: `--env-var`, `--tools`, `--usage` and `--confirm`.\n  - `set` changes only the fields you pass and keeps the rest of the file, comments included. `--unset FIELD` removes a field. It fails if the task doesn't exist.\n  - `rm` (alias `remove`) deletes a TOML task's table or a file task's script. It asks for confirmation unless you pass `--yes`, and it refuses tasks inferred from `package.json` or defined in included files.\n  - All three commands accept `--json` and `--dry-run`. If more than one loaded file defines a task, `set` and `rm` stop and list the files so you can choose one.\n  - When `add` or `set` trusts the config root it writes to, it now says so on stderr.\n\n  ```sh\n  mise tasks add --env local deploy --env-var STAGE=prod --tools node@24 \\\n    --confirm \"Deploy now?\" -- ./deploy.sh\n  mise tasks set build --description \"Build for release\" -- cargo build --release\n  mise tasks rm --env local --yes scratch\n  ```\n\n- **`mise watch --once`.** mise waits for a change in the watched sources, runs the tasks once with `mise run`, and exits with their status. It uses the same filters as a normal watch and runs nothing at start, so `--postpone` is implied. Flags that only control how watchexec reruns the command, such as `--restart` and `--clear`, are refused with `--once`. Ctrl-C exits with 130. SIGTERM while waiting exits without running anything. A signal during the run stops the whole task process tree. `mise watch` also now passes `--exit-on-error` to watchexec, which stops at the first failed run and exits with its status. `--exit-on-error` requires watchexec 2.5.0 or later. [#15212](https://github.com/jdx/mise/pull/15212) (@jdx)\n\n  ```sh\n  mise watch test --once && echo \"tests pass after the change\"\n  ```\n\n- **`env_cache_redacted` setting.** With `env_cache = true`, mise never caches an environment that contains a `redact = true` directive, so a slow `_.source` script ran again on every prompt. The new opt-in `env_cache_redacted` setting (`MISE_ENV_CACHE_REDACTED=1`, default `false`) lets these environments be cached. Values stay masked in output when they are read from the cache. The tradeoff: the secret values are stored on disk, encrypted with a key that every process started from the activated shell inherits. `age` and sops values, plugin results with `cacheable = false`, redacted `[vars]`, and redacted `default`/`required` directives are still never cached. With `MISE_DEBUG=1`, mise now logs when an environment wasn't cached and which directive prevented it. [#15200](https://github.com/jdx/mise/pull/15200) (@jdx, @Achder)\n\n  ```toml\n  [settings]\n  env_cache = true\n  env_cache_redacted = true\n  ```\n\n- **`managed_by` in bootstrap package status JSON.** Installed `brew-cask` packages now report `managed_by` (`\"mise\"`, `\"homebrew\"` or `\"unknown\"`) in `mise bootstrap packages status --json` and `mise bootstrap status --json`. Scripts can use it to tell which casks mise will upgrade and which it leaves to Homebrew. [#15198](https://github.com/jdx/mise/pull/15198) (@jdx, @drewbitt)\n- **Registry:** added `jouet`, which installs from its signed packslip [#15201](https://github.com/jdx/mise/pull/15201) (@jdx)\n\n## Changed\n\n- `mise tasks add` now accepts `--force`. Re-adding an identical task does nothing. Replacing a task that has a different definition without `--force` still works for now. mise will warn about it starting in 2027.5.0 and refuse it starting in 2028.5.0. Scripts that only run on current mise versions can add `--force` now. [#15213](https://github.com/jdx/mise/pull/15213) (@jdx)\n\n## Fixed\n\n- Running several `mise install` processes at once could fail with errors like \"Python graph installs require an installed interpreter\", because one process saw a tool another process was still installing and treated it as complete. Installs and forced reinstalls now mark a version as incomplete before touching its directory. [#15196](https://github.com/jdx/mise/pull/15196) (@jdx, @theredspoon)\n- When `~/.config/mise` is a symlink into a dotfiles checkout that has a `config.override.toml`, running `mise install` inside the checkout no longer makes `mise.lock` alternate between full and empty. [#15197](https://github.com/jdx/mise/pull/15197) (@jdx, @ponko2)\n- `mise reshim`, including the reshim at the end of `mise install`, no longer slows down quadratically with the number of tracked configs. On machines with thousands of tracked projects it could take minutes. [#15203](https://github.com/jdx/mise/pull/15203) (@jdx, @Birzool)\n- On macOS, signed `.app` bundles extracted from tarballs made with bsdtar are no longer killed on launch. mise now applies the `._*` AppleDouble files to the files they belong to and then deletes them, as macOS `tar` does. [#15211](https://github.com/jdx/mise/pull/15211) (@jdx, @jpeeler)\n- A `[bootstrap.repos]` entry whose `ref` is an annotated tag now reports `current` once that tag is checked out. Before, it always reported `differs`, and every `apply` checked the tag out again. [#15199](https://github.com/jdx/mise/pull/15199) (@jdx)\n- Dotfiles: replacing several tracked paths with the directory that contains them no longer leaves the directory stuck as \"not applied here\" with autosave stopped. [#15187](https://github.com/jdx/mise/pull/15187) (@jdx, @drewbitt)\n- Dotfiles: `mise dot untrack` with several paths now checks all of them before changing anything. Before, if a later path wasn't tracked, the command failed but had already stopped tracking the earlier paths. [#15208](https://github.com/jdx/mise/pull/15208) (@jdx, @drewbitt)\n\n**Full Changelog**: https://github.com/jdx/mise/compare/v2026.10.24...v2026.10.25\n\n## 💚 Sponsor mise\n\nmise is built and maintained by [@jdx](https://github.com/jdx), an open source developer at [**entire.io**](https://entire.io/), the title sponsor of his open source work.\n\nIf mise saves you or your team time, please consider becoming an [individual or company sponsor](https://jdx.dev/sponsors.html). Your support funds ongoing development and helps keep mise fast, free, and independent.\n","reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mise/releases/429861984/reactions","total_count":2,"+1":1,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":1,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"mentions_count":7}